Tasks

1. In a MongoDB collection named playlists (think Spotify), use the $set operator to update the 'genre' field to 'Chill' for the playlist with name 'Evening Vibes'.

->  db.playlists.updateOne( { name: "Evening Vibes" }, { $set: { genre: "Chill" } } )


2. For a collection called restaurants (Zomato-style), remove the 'temporaryOffer' field from all documents where the offer has expired using the $unset operator.

->  db.restaurants.updateMany( { offerExpired: true }, { $unset: { temporaryOffer: "" } } )


3. Using the $push operator, add a new song 'Calm Waters' to the 'songs' array of the playlist document with _id: 101 in your playlists collection.

->  db.playlists.updateOne( { _id: 101 }, { $push: { songs: "Calm Waters" } } )


4. In a Flipkart-like products collection, use the $addToSet operator to ensure the tag 'trending' is added only once to the 'tags' array for the product with SKU 'F12345', even if you run the update multiple times.

->  db.products.updateOne( { SKU: "F12345" }, { $addToSet: { tags: "trending" } } )


5. Simulate a flash sale: in your products collection, use the $inc operator to decrease the 'stock' field by 1 for the product with name 'Wireless Earbuds' every time a purchase is made

->  db.products.updateOne( { name: "Wireless Earbuds", stock: { $gt: 0 } }, { $inc: { stock: -1 } } )